## First use scenario
At the beginning of the project, there was only one module for adding products. Due to business iteration, video products were separated from multi-picture products. Current problems:
1. Version iteration will inevitably modify both modules at the same time;
1. A lot of logic and repeated methods.
## Solution filtering
In view of the above problems, the solutions sought are:
1. Extract public logic code;
1. Extract public container components;
1. Mixin;
1. High-order components.
Public logic extraction solution, public logic includes state, props, and setState. If the public logic code is extracted, the relevant state will inevitably be reprocessed, which will be very destructive to the original module.
The public container component solution merges the common parts of the two modules, separates the upload part into multi-image upload components, and the video upload component distinguishes types by passing in parameters, which means merging route names, adding parameter distinctions, and high modification costs.
Mixin solution, but its solution is flawed. For details, see here
The best solution that can be chosen in the end is high-order components, which are less intrusive and have functional thinking (the same input, returns the same output, and is highly predictable).
## Concept introduction
### Higher-order functions
It satisfies at least two conditions for higher-order functions:
- Accepts one or more functions as input
- Output a function
### High-order components
Definition: Analogous to the definition of a higher-order function, a higher-order component accepts a component as a parameter, performs a series of processes on the component in the function, and then returns a new component as the return value.
## Interpretation of high-order components
Example 1 (Property Proxy Pattern)
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
      return <WrappedComponent {...this.props}/>
    }
  }
}
```
Example 2 (reverse inheritance pattern)
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends WrappedComponent {
    render() {
      return super.render();
    }
  }
}
```
What? Attribute proxy, reverse inheritance, forget about inheritance, why do you still have reverse inheritance? Don’t rush to read on.
### Property proxy
To put it simply, it means wrapping components and operating props. Basically, this component nesting mode and parameter passing method are often used.
Up code
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
      return <WrappedComponent {...this.props}/>
    }
  }
}

class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        //....
    }
}

//Use of high-order components
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```
### Attribute operations
Up code
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
        const newProps = {
            name: "HOC"
        }
      return <WrappedComponent {...this.props} {...newProps}/>
    }
  }
}

class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        //....
    }
}

//Use of high-order components
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```
I have basically done component parameter passing.
### refs reference
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    render() {
      return <WrappedComponent ref={this.onRef} {...this.props}/>
    }
  }
}
```
Basically, I have done this before, using component instance methods.
### Abstract State
Up code
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends Component {
    onChange = (data = {}) => {
        this.setState(data)
    }
    render() {
        const {name = ''} = this.state;
        const newProps = {
            name: {
                value: name,
                onChange: (e)=>this.onChange({name: e.target.value})
            }
        }
        return <WrappedComponent {...this.props} {...newProps}/>
    }
  }
}

class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        const {name} = this.props;
       return <input {...name} />
    }
}

//Use of high-order components
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```
Carefully appreciate the essence of the above writing method, and you will find its benefits when you need to operate a large number of forms.
I didn’t understand the meaning of abstract State at first, but I figured it out by writing notes. It is to abstract the operations that need to be dynamically assigned through state in the original component to high-order components to pass values ​​through props.
Summary: The attribute proxy mode is similar to the usual mode of writing components, but with some added techniques.
### Reverse inheritance
Look at the following example again
```plain text
const HOC = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return class extends WrappedComponent {
    render() {
      return super.render();
    }
  }
}
```
The super.render() mode only passes super.method() when the es6 class inherits. It is really wonderful to use here.
From the writing point of view, after inheriting the incoming component, using super.render() naturally executes the render method of the incoming component, that is, rendering the page corresponding to the incoming component. The interesting things begin.
### Manipulate state and props.
I understand operating state, but operating props, what the hell, until I see this piece of code:
