### Render hijacking
Code written by others
```plain text
//The example comes from "In-depth React Technology Stack"

const HOC = (WrappedComponent) =>
    class extends WrappedComponent {
        render() {
            const elementsTree = super.render();
            let newProps = {};
            if (elementsTree && elementsTree.type === 'input') {
                newProps = {value: 'may the force be with you'};
            }
            const props = Object.assign({}, elementsTree.props, newProps);
            const newElementsTree = React.cloneElement(elementsTree, props, elementsTree.props.children);
            return newElementsTree;
    }
}
class WrappedComponent extends Component{
    render(){
        return(
            <input value={'Hello World'} />
        )
    }
}
export default HOC(WrappedComponent)
```
It is similar to operating DOM (personal understanding). There is currently no scenario where this is used, so I won’t go into details.
Now that I have introduced the basic usage scenarios of high-end components, let’s get back to the topic and talk about how I use them in actual projects.
Let’s talk about some data:
Before modifying the module:
Multi-picture product module code, 900 lines. Video product module code, 1000 lines
After modifying the module:
Multi-picture product module code, 554 lines. Video product module code, 650 lines
387 lines of extracted high-order component code
The current level can only go so far.
I adopted the reverse inheritance scheme, attribute proxy, which can only operate props, but cannot operate state. You may have doubts that state cannot be abstracted. Yes, see clearly, it is abstraction and not operation.
Also, the attribute proxy cannot use the static methods of the original component. The master provides a library for batch copying static methods hoist-non-react-statics
The feasible solution that finally satisfies me in manipulating props, especially state, is reverse inheritance of higher-order components.
